# Project: Jest JavaScript Unit Test Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use Jest as the primary test runner for JavaScript/TypeScript projects
- Organize tests in __tests__ directories or co-locate with source files using .test.js/.spec.js suffix
- Use TypeScript with ts-jest or Babel for transpilation
- Configure jest.config.js (or jest.config.ts) as the single source of truth for test settings

## Coding Standards
- Follow JavaScript/TypeScript best practices
- Use meaningful, descriptive test and variable names
- Implement clean code principles
- Limit describe block nesting to 3 levels maximum
- Keep each test file focused on a single module or component

## Test Organization
- Group related tests using describe blocks
- Use it/test functions for individual test cases
- Implement beforeEach/afterEach for per-test setup and teardown
- Use beforeAll/afterAll only for expensive shared resources that are truly read-only
- Separate unit, integration, and snapshot tests into distinct directories

## Best Practices
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern consistently
- Write focused, single-responsibility tests
- Avoid test interdependencies and shared mutable state between tests
- Use jest.fn() for mocking instead of manual stubs
- Clear or reset mocks in afterEach to prevent cross-test pollution
- Prefer explicit assertions over implicit pass-by-not-throwing

## Mocking Strategies
- Use jest.mock() for module-level mocking with automatic hoisting
- Implement jest.spyOn() for method-level spying without replacing the full module
- Use jest.useFakeTimers() with modern mode for timer-dependent tests
- Mock HTTP requests with msw (Mock Service Worker) for realistic network simulation
- Restore all mocks after each test with jest.restoreAllMocks() or clearMocks: true in config
- Prefer __mocks__ directories for frequently mocked modules

## Assertions
- Use expect() with specific, descriptive matchers from @jest/expect
- Implement custom matchers via expect.extend() for domain-specific assertions
- Use toMatchSnapshot() and toMatchInlineSnapshot() only for stable, deterministic output
- Prefer toEqual for deep equality over toBe for object/array comparisons
- Use toThrow and toThrowError with specific error messages or types

## Coverage
- Configure coverage collection with the coverageProvider option (v8 recommended for accuracy)
- Set meaningful thresholds in coverageThreshold for statements, branches, functions, and lines
- Use coveragePathIgnorePatterns to exclude generated files, mocks, and test utilities
- Generate lcov and html reports: lcov for CI integration, html for local visualization
- Run coverage in CI but not in watch mode to keep local feedback fast

## Performance
- Enable --runInBand only for debugging; prefer parallel workers in CI
- Use testPathPattern or testNamePattern to run targeted subsets during development
- Minimize expensive beforeAll operations; prefer lightweight beforeEach
- Configure maxWorkers in jest.config to balance CPU usage in CI environments
- Use --bail to stop early on first failure in pre-commit hooks

## Snapshot Testing
- Keep snapshot files small and focused; avoid snapshotting entire page trees
- Update snapshots intentionally with --updateSnapshot, review diffs in code review
- Use toMatchInlineSnapshot() for small, self-documenting snapshots in the test file
- Delete obsolete snapshots with --ci flag to prevent stale files from accumulating

## Test Data Management
- Use factory functions or builders for reusable, composable test data
- Keep fixtures in a dedicated __fixtures__ directory
- Avoid hardcoding IDs, dates, and environment-specific values; use constants or generators
- Use faker or similar libraries for randomized but realistic data generation

## Error Handling
- Test both the happy path and error/edge cases for every public function
- Assert on error messages and error types, not just that an error was thrown
- Test async rejection paths with rejects.toThrow() or try/catch inside tests
- Use expect.assertions(n) in async tests to catch silent resolution instead of rejection

## CI/CD Integration
- Run jest with --ci flag in pipelines to disable interactive prompts and fail on missing snapshots
- Output results in JUnit XML format (jest-junit reporter) for CI test result parsing
- Cache node_modules and Jest's transform cache between pipeline runs for speed
- Run linting and type-checking in parallel with the test suite, not sequentially
- Use --forceExit if test environment teardown hangs in CI

## Documentation
- Use JSDoc comments on test helpers and custom matchers to clarify intent
- Document non-obvious test setup steps in a comment at the top of the test file
- Maintain a README.md in the tests root explaining directory structure and conventions

Remember to keep tests fast, deterministic, and independent — a test suite that is slow or flaky will be ignored, undermining the entire testing strategy.